# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
# (`.next/` itself is covered by the `.next*/` rule at the foot of this file.)
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Impeccable (design tooling) — machine-local overrides
.impeccable/config.local.json
.impeccable/live/
.impeccable/*cache*

# Editor/agent backups — never tracked. One was swept in by a `git add -A` on 2026-08-31.
*.bak

# UI screenshots from `npm run ui:shots` — evidence for a change report, never committed
.artifacts/

# EVERY Next build directory, whatever it is called — never committed.
#
# One rule, not a list of names. This started as `/.next/`, then `.next-preview/` was added for the
# screenshot lane (it must not contend with `.next`, see next.config.ts), then `.next-probe/` and
# `.next-probe2/` for ad-hoc `next build` typecheck runs, then `.next-probe*/` on 2026-09-12 when a
# third one slipped through — and within the day `.next-guestwall/` and `.next-gw2/` were created
# and matched none of them. Each new name is a full build tree sitting untracked in `git status`
# waiting for somebody's `git add -A`.
#
# The trailing slash matters: it matches DIRECTORIES only, so `next-env.d.ts` (ignored above, no
# leading dot) and any future `.next-something.json` file are unaffected.
.next*/
